[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Shortest Path Algorithms: Dijkstra's and Bellman-Ford"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Shortest path algorithms are fundamental in computer science and graph theory. They aim to find the path with the minimum weight (cost) between two vertices in a graph. This tutorial covers two prominent algorithms: Dijkstra's algorithm and the Bellman-Ford algorithm."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Dijkstra's Algorithm"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dijkstra's algorithm is a greedy algorithm used to find the shortest paths from a single source vertex to all other vertices in a graph. It works by iteratively selecting the vertex with the smallest known distance from the source and updating the distances to its neighbors. Dijkstra's algorithm requires non-negative edge weights."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Suitable for graphs with non-negative edge weights."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Greedy approach: always selects the closest unvisited vertex."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single-source shortest path algorithm."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm Steps"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"1. Initialize distances: Assign a distance value to all vertices in the graph. Set the distance to the source vertex to 0 and infinity to all other vertices."},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"2. Maintain a set of visited vertices. Initially, this set is empty."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"3. While there are unvisited vertices:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"a. Select the unvisited vertex with the smallest distance (current vertex)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"b. Mark the current vertex as visited."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"c. For each neighbor of the current vertex:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"i. Calculate the distance to the neighbor through the current vertex."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ii. If this distance is less than the current distance to the neighbor, update the neighbor's distance."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"function dijkstra(graph, source) {
  const dist = {};
  const visited = new Set();
  for (let node in graph) {
    dist[node] = Infinity;
  }
  dist[source] = 0;

  while (true) {
    let closestNode = null;
    let closestDistance = Infinity;

    for (let node in graph) {
      if (!visited.has(node) && dist[node] < closestDistance) {
        closestNode = node;
        closestDistance = dist[node];
      }
    }

    if (closestNode === null) {
      break;
    }

    visited.add(closestNode);

    for (let neighbor in graph[closestNode]) {
      const distance = graph[closestNode][neighbor];
      if (dist[closestNode] + distance < dist[neighbor]) {
        dist[neighbor] = dist[closestNode] + distance;
      }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Bellman-Ford Algorithm"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Bellman-Ford algorithm is another single-source shortest path algorithm that can handle graphs with negative edge weights. It works by repeatedly relaxing the edges of the graph. Relaxation is the process of updating the distance to a vertex if a shorter path is found."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handles graphs with negative edge weights."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Detects negative cycles (cycles with a negative total weight)."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single-source shortest path algorithm."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm Steps"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"1. Initialize distances: Assign a distance value to all vertices in the graph. Set the distance to the source vertex to 0 and infinity to all other vertices."},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"2. Relax edges repeatedly: For each edge (u, v) in the graph, relax the edge |V| - 1 times, where |V| is the number of vertices in the graph. Relaxation means checking if the distance to v can be shortened by going through u."},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"3. Check for negative cycles: After |V| - 1 iterations, perform one more iteration of edge relaxation. If any distance is still updated, it indicates the presence of a negative cycle."},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"function bellmanFord(graph, source) {
  const dist = {};
  for (let node in graph) {
    dist[node] = Infinity;
  }
  dist[source] = 0;

  const edges = [];
  for (let node in graph) {
    for (let neighbor in graph[node]) {
      edges.push([node, neighbor, graph[node][neighbor]]);
    }
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < Object.keys(graph).length - 1; i++) {
    for (let edge of edges) {
      const [u, v, weight] = edge;
      if (dist[u] !== Infinity && dist[u] + weight < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + weight;
      }
    }
  }

  // Check for negative cycles
  for (let edge of edges) {
    const [u, v, weight] = edge;
    if (dist[u] !== Infinity && dist[u] + weight < dist[v]) {
      return 'Negative cycle detected';
    }
  }

  return dist;
}"},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dijkstra's: Faster for graphs with non-negative edge weights (O(E log V) with a priority queue)."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bellman-Ford: Slower (O(V*E)), but can handle negative edge weights and detect negative cycles."},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dijkstra's and Bellman-Ford are essential shortest path algorithms with different strengths and weaknesses. Dijkstra's is more efficient for graphs with non-negative edge weights, while Bellman-Ford can handle negative edge weights and detect negative cycles. The choice of algorithm depends on the specific characteristics of the graph and the requirements of the application."}]
